The proposed answer states that the first televised Academy Awards ceremony was in 1952, when the 24th Academy Awards (honoring films of 1951) was broadcast live, hosted by Bob Hope, with "An American in Paris" winning Best Picture.

Checking these supporting claims: The 24th Academy Awards took place on March 20, 1952, and was indeed the first Oscars ceremony to be televised. Bob Hope hosted, and "An American in Paris" won Best Picture. All supporting details align with known facts, and the core answer of 1952 is correct.

{"verdict": "correct", "confidence": 95, "correct_answer": "1952"}

The proposed answer is incorrect. While Richard Donner did shoot the majority of *Superman II* footage during the simultaneous production with the first film, he was fired from the production and was **not** credited as director on the theatrical 1980 release. Richard Lester, who had been brought in as an uncredited producer/second unit facilitator on the first film (partly to satisfy the Salkinds' contractual obligations regarding credit disputes), took over and reshot significant portions of the film. The theatrical cut of *Superman II* is credited to Richard Lester. Donner only received directorial credit years later with the 2006 "Richard Donner Cut."

The proposed answer's claim that "the film as released in theaters in 1980 carries Donner's directorial authorship" is factually wrong — the on-screen credit in 1980 reads "Directed by Richard Lester."

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 99, "correct_answer": "Richard Lester"}

The proposed answer contains an error. The American Professional Football Association (APFA) did indeed change its name to the National Football League, but this occurred in **1922**, not 1921. The league was founded in 1920 in Canton, Ohio (that part is correct), and Joe Carr became president in 1921 (also correct), but the official renaming happened at the league meeting on June 24, 1922.

The supporting claims about the founding year (1920), the founding location (Canton, Ohio), and Joe Carr's presidency beginning in 1921 are accurate, but the central claim — the year of the name change — is wrong.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 95, "correct_answer": "1922"}

The proposed answer is incorrect. The game was invented by **Alfred Mosher Butts**, an architect from New York, who developed it during the Great Depression (early-to-mid 1930s) first as **Lexiko**, then as **Criss-Cross Words**. Butts famously analyzed letter frequencies in newspapers to design the tile distribution and point values.

James Brunot's actual role was different from what the proposed answer claims: Butts turned the game over to Brunot (a friend who had been one of its early players) in 1948. Brunot renamed it **Scrabble**, made minor rule refinements, trademarked it, and manufactured it through his company, Production and Marketing. The Macy's/Jack Straus story and the Selchow & Righter licensing did happen — but those pertain to Brunot's *commercialization* of the game, not its invention. The proposed answer conflates the inventor with the marketer.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 98, "correct_answer": "Alfred Mosher Butts"}

The proposed answer contains a critical factual error. While Chuck Cooper was indeed the first African American **drafted** by an NBA team, he was not the first to play in a regular-season game. Earl Lloyd of the Washington Capitols debuted on October 31, 1950, one day before Cooper's Celtics debut (November 1, 1950). Nat Clifton (Knicks) debuted a few days later. So the first African American to appear in an NBA regular-season game was Earl Lloyd.

Additionally, some details in the answer are suspect: the draft was BAA in 1950 (it merged with NBL to become NBA in August 1949), Cooper averaged about 6.7 points per game (roughly correct), but the "first regular-season game" claim is wrong.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 90, "correct_answer": "Earl Lloyd"}

The proposed answer contains a significant factual error. Dolly the sheep was born on **July 5, 1996** at the Roslin Institute. What happened in 1997 was the *announcement* of her birth — the paper in *Nature* (by Wilmut, Campbell et al.) was published in February 1997, which is when the public learned of her existence, roughly seven months after her actual birth.

The proposed answer inverts the actual facts: it claims 1997 is the birth year and dismisses 1996 as a "common point of confusion" related to "preliminary embryo work," when in fact 1996 is the correct birth year and the confusion arises precisely because the announcement came in 1997. Other details (Roslin Institute, Ian Wilmut and Keith Campbell, somatic cell nuclear transfer from a mammary gland cell, euthanized in 2003) are accurate, but the central claim — the birth year — is wrong.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 98, "correct_answer": "1996"}

The proposed answer is incorrect. The 1957 Plano Piloto competition for Brasília was won by **Lúcio Costa**, whose entry (submitted under the motto "Erasmo" or numbered anonymously) proposed the crossed-axis design — the monumental east-west axis intersected by the residential north-south axis, often compared to an airplane or bird in flight. Oscar Niemeyer, Costa's frequent collaborator, designed many of the city's major buildings (Congresso Nacional, Palácio da Alvorada, Palácio do Planalto, Supremo Tribunal Federal, Catedral, etc.), which is a common source of confusion, but he did not win the urban planning competition.

The proposed answer inverts the roles: it attributes the winning master plan to Niemeyer and casts Costa as a peripheral figure who merely served on a technical commission. In reality, Costa's Plano Piloto is the celebrated winning urban design, and Niemeyer's contribution was architectural. The answer is factually wrong on its central claim.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 99, "correct_answer": "Lúcio Costa"}

The proposed answer contains an error. Robert Koch announced his discovery of the tubercle bacillus to the Berlin Physiological Society on March 24, **1882**, not 1883. The answer's date of March 24 is correct, but the year is off by one.

Other details: The lecture is indeed regarded as a landmark in medical history, and Koch was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1905 — those claims are accurate. But the year of the announcement is wrong.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 99, "correct_answer": "1882"}

The proposed answer is incorrect and internally contradictory. The historical fact is well-established: Louis Pasteur was born on December 27, 1822, in Dole, in the Jura department of eastern France. His family moved to Arbois around 1826 (when he was about three or four years old), and Arbois is indeed where he grew up, where the Maison de Pasteur museum stands, and where his father's tannery was located. But Arbois is his childhood home, not his birthplace.

The proposed answer even acknowledges the correct fact at the beginning ("Louis Pasteur was born in Dole..."), then bizarrely reverses itself and concludes that he was "actually born" in Arbois, which is false. The final answer given — Arbois — is wrong. The correct answer is Dole.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 99, "correct_answer": "Dole"}

The proposed answer is incorrect. The patient who received the world's first human-to-human heart transplant, performed by Christiaan Barnard at Groote Schuur Hospital on December 3, 1967, was **Louis Washkansky**, a 54-year-old grocer. Washkansky survived only 18 days, dying of pneumonia caused by his immunosuppressed state.

Philip Blaiberg was the recipient of the *second* human heart transplant, performed by Barnard in January 1968 — not December 1967. The answer's claims about Blaiberg being a retired dentist and about Denise Darvall as the first donor (which is accurate — she was Washkansky's donor) are misplaced, as Blaiberg's donor was Clive Haupt. The entire framing that Blaiberg was the December 1967 first recipient is wrong.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 99, "correct_answer": "Louis Washkansky"}

The proposed answer is incorrect. The 1902 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine was awarded to **Ronald Ross**, a British Army physician working in India, for his 1897–1898 discovery that mosquitoes transmit malaria parasites (specifically, that female *Anopheles* mosquitoes carry the parasite between humans — he demonstrated the parasite's presence in the mosquito's gut and its transmission cycle in birds).

While Giovanni Battista Grassi made important contributions, particularly in identifying *Anopheles* as the specific vector and demonstrating human transmission, it was Ross who received the Nobel Prize. The answer contains several errors, including fabricated details (e.g., the hut experiment framing) and a misspelling ("Grassasi"). Grassi was famously embittered that Ross alone received the prize, as their priority dispute was well documented.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 99, "correct_answer": "Ronald Ross"}

The proposed answer contains errors. The OECD Convention was signed in December 1960 by 20 countries, and it entered into force on 30 September 1961. The founding signatories included the United States and Canada alongside the European OEEC participants — but the count was 20, not 18. The answer's list omits two founding members: Greece and Spain (Spain was a founding signatory, having signed in 1960, even though its ratification came later — but it is counted among the founding members). Greece, omitted from the proposed list, was clearly one of the original OEEC participants and an OECD founding signatory.

The claim that Spain "joined later" is wrong — Spain signed the OECD Convention in 1960 as a founding member (though it ratified in September 1961, just before entry into force). Japan indeed joined later (1964), but that does not reduce the founding count to 18.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 95, "correct_answer": "20"}

The proposed answer is incorrect. While it's true that Roosevelt sent wage-and-hour legislation to Congress in May 1937, that bill (and subsequent versions) faced significant opposition—both from Southern Democrats and through Supreme Court concerns—and did not pass that session. The Fair Labor Standards Act was actually passed by Congress in 1938 and signed into law by President Roosevelt on June 25, 1938. The act's provisions (minimum wage of 25 cents per hour, the 44-hour work week scheduled to drop to 40, and child labor restrictions) took effect in October 1938, so the claim that enforcement beginning in 1938 is a source of confusion is itself a fabricated rationalization. The act is universally dated to 1938—the year it was signed into law.

The proposed answer contains a plausible-sounding but false narrative structure (bill introduced 1937, signed 1937, enforcement 1938), when the reality is simply: introduced 1937, signed 1938, effective October 1938.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 99, "correct_answer": "1938"}

The proposed answer is incorrect.

On 1 January 1999, **eleven** EU member states adopted the euro: Austria, Belgium, Finland, France, Germany, Ireland, Italy, Luxembourg, the Netherlands, Portugal, and Spain. Greece did **not** adopt the euro on that date — it failed to meet the convergence criteria in the 1998 assessment and only joined the euro area on **1 January 2001**, ahead of the physical cash introduction in 2002. The proposed answer contains a factual error, attempting to rationalize Greece's inclusion by claiming its entry was "part of the original EMU framework," which is false — Greece was explicitly excluded from the founding cohort and joined two years later after being assessed separately.

{"verdict": "incorrect", "confidence": 99, "correct_answer": "11"}
